.TH CTPOOL_SUBMIT_FUTURE 3 "2026-09-21" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_submit_future \- submit a task that returns a void* result (blocking)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

ctpool_future *ctpool_submit_future(ctpool pool,
                                    void *(*fn)(void *),
                                    void *arg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates a future object and submits a task to the pool. The task
calls fn(arg) on a worker thread and stores the void* pointer that fn
returned in the future, so that ctpool_future_get can read it later.

On success the future has a reference count of 2: the caller holds one
reference, and the task in the queue holds the other inside the
library. Each ctpool_future_get call that runs on the future adds one
more. The caller must call ctpool_future_free exactly one time when it
is done.

For a bounded queue this function blocks until the queue has a free
slot; use ctpool_try_submit_future or ctpool_timed_submit_future when
you do not want it to block.

A call from a task that runs on this same pool, or from the on_complete
callback of that task, while a bounded queue is already at capacity,
returns NULL instead of waiting, for the reason that ctpool_submit(3)
gives: only the workers of this pool can free a slot, and the caller is
one of them. ctpool_try_submit_future(3) and
ctpool_timed_submit_future(3) report that case as ccol_container_full,
and an unbounded queue never reaches it at all.

A future always uses the standard malloc and free, not a custom
allocator that the caller set at pool creation.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
pool  Thread pool handle
fn    Task function returning void* (must not be NULL)
arg   Passed to fn (may be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new ctpool_future handle on success.
Returns NULL in these five cases:
  pool is CTPOOL_INVALID, or a stale and already destroyed handle
  fn is NULL
  the pool is in shutdown
  a bounded queue is at capacity and the caller is a worker of this
    same pool
  a memory allocation fails
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_try_submit_future (3),
.BR ctpool_timed_submit_future (3),
.BR ctpool_future_get (3),
.BR ctpool_future_done (3),
.BR ctpool_future_cancelled (3),
.BR ctpool_future_free (3)
